course: dictionary
lesson: 8509
title: A satchel: a bag for carrying
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A satchel is a bag with a long strap. You can wear it over your shoulder or on your back, and it is used especially for carrying school books.
explain: Context helps you find this meaning. The Rat «packs a satchel, takes a stick and steps out of his door». You pack things into a bag, so a satchel must be something you can fill and carry.
explain: The word feels old-fashioned. In the UK, Ireland and Australia, satchels were traditional leather school bags, and old poems still describe a schoolboy going «with his satchel and shining morning face». Today most pupils use backpacks or rucksacks.
fact: A satchel is a bag with a long strap, worn over the shoulder or on the back.
fact: Satchels were used especially for carrying school books.
fact: «Satchel» is a noun: one satchel, two satchels.
fact: In the UK, Ireland and Australia, satchels were the traditional leather school bags.
fact: Today most pupils use backpacks or rucksacks, but the word satchel still gives an old-fashioned school feeling.
exercise: What is a satchel? | A type of hat worn by walkers | A small cup used for drinking tea | *A bag with a long strap, worn over the shoulder or on the back
exercise: In «the Rat packs a satchel, takes a stick and steps out of his door», what is the Rat doing with the satchel? | Hanging it on a tree | *Packing things into it to carry | Sleeping on it | Eating from it
exercise: Which of these would a pupil most likely carry in a satchel? | *Maths and reading books | A pair of wellies | A bowl of soup | A bicycle
test: Which word means a bag with a long strap, worn over the shoulder? | *Satchel | Kettle | Glove | Pillow
test: In old poems, a schoolboy goes «with his satchel and shining morning face». What does the satchel suggest? | A visit to a shop | A trip to the beach | A rainy night in town | *An old-fashioned school feeling
exam: The Rat «packs a satchel, takes a stick and steps out of his door». What does packing a satchel most likely mean? | Painting a picture | *Filling a bag with things to carry | Sleeping in a bed | Eating a meal
